<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aulic Council</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1497">1497</a>–<a href="/years/1806">1806</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Imperial Aulic Council, Reichshofrat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Aulic Council, or Reichshofrat, was one of the two supreme courts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-chamber-court">Imperial Chamber Court</a>. Sitting at the emperor's court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, it heard disputes between imperial estates and feudal cases. Its sentences could place cities and princes under the imperial ban, as with Donauwörth in 1607.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aulic-council.jpg" alt="Aulic Council" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gryffindor · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reichskanzleitrakt_Vienna_Sept_2006_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The council grew out of the paid council of the emperor in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-middle-ages">Late Middle Ages</a> and was given its later form by decree of Maximilian I, as a rival to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-chamber-court">Imperial Chamber Court</a> that the Imperial Estates had established at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diet-of-worms">Diet of Worms</a> shortly before. Its name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> aula, court. Unlike the Chamber Court, which was tied to the estates, it was bound directly to the emperor and moved with his residences: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, Wels and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> all housed it, but its most important seat was the Hofburg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under Rudolf II, in the late 1500s and early 1600s, it often handled conflicts the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a> could not settle, many of them religious, and Protestant princes criticised it for siding with the Catholics. From 1620 it heard far more cases than the Chamber Court, and in 1654 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-holy-roman-emperor">Ferdinand III</a> balanced its Protestant and Catholic members under pressure from the Diet. It intervened in the constitutions of Imperial Cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>, placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a> under the Imperial Ban before the Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> subdued the city in 1664, and led to the deposition of Prince William Hyacinth of Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siegen">Siegen</a> in 1707.</p><p>Historians speak of juridification to describe how its work helped settle disputes in the Empire by law rather than violence. Eva Ortlieb ranks it with the Rota Romana and the Parlement de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> among the most significant supreme courts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and more than 100,000 of its case files still exist.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Each emperor summoned a new council on his accession, normally of 10 to 25 members whom he paid. A noble president appointed by the emperor moderated sessions and held the deciding vote, and the Vice Chancellor appointed by the Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> carried great weight. Councillors sat on a Lords' Bench and a Knights' and Scholars' Bench but decided as one body by majority vote. Its rulings could be challenged only before the emperor or later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a>. Through its Debit Commissions it could take over the government of a prince who defaulted, and it provided counsel free of charge to poor litigants.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aulic Council” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1606 Protestant townspeople attacked a Catholic procession, and the <b>Imperial Aulic Council</b> placed the city under the imperial ban.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-donauworth">Free Imperial City of Donauwörth</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1669 the <b>Imperial Aulic Council</b> confirmed the Archbishop of Trier's lordship, ending the abbey's claim to independence.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-saint-maximin">Imperial Abbey of Saint Maximin</a></li></ul></section>
